  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Paraguay is South American football's great survivor \u2014 a landlocked nation that has qualified for nine World Cups through sheer defensive grit and collective organization, usually with a roster that punches well above its individual talent level.",
    "footballIdentity": "In a continent where football is theology, Paraguay has always had a different gospel: don't lose. The Guaran\u00ed spirit \u2014 resilient, proud, deeply communal \u2014 runs through every defensive block and every last-ditch tackle. Where Brazil dazzles and Argentina inspires, Paraguay grinds, endures, and occasionally stuns. The 2010 squad, which reached the quarterfinals by beating Japan before losing narrowly to eventual champions Spain, remains the gold standard.",
    "theMoment": "After missing 2018 and 2022, Paraguay returns to the World Cup with Alfaro's well-organized defensive structure and expectations refreshingly reset. In a brutal Group D that includes the hosts, this is a team that only needs to stay compact \u2014 three opponents good enough that merely keeping games tight until the final twenty minutes is already a minor victory in itself.",
    "momentType": "footballing"
  },
  "keyPlayers": [
    {
      "name": "Miguel Almir\u00f3n",
      "club": "Atlanta United",
      "position": "Attacking Midfielder / Right Wing",
      "badge": "Captain",
      "note": "The elder statesman of this squad and its heartbeat. After years at Newcastle where he became a fan legend for his tireless running, Almir\u00f3n returned to MLS to finish his career but his commitment to the national team has never wavered. His energy and experience provide exactly what a counter-attacking side needs."
    },
